# SelfHost Contribution Guidelines

## SelfHost Development Workflow
- **Local Development**: Test all changes locally first using `podman-setup.sh`
- **Server Deployment**: Use `scp` to copy changes to `legion@aevion:~/selfhost/`
- **Service Management**: Use `systemctl --user <action> <service-name>` for service control
- **Verification**: Check `podman logs <container-name>` and service status after deployment
- **Documentation**: Update relevant README files and deployment notes

## Adding New Services
- **Script-Driven Development**: Edit `podman-setup.sh` scripts - they generate quadlet files automatically
- **Quadlets Are Generated**: Never create/edit `.container` files manually - run scripts to generate them
- **Scripts Handle Everything**: `podman-setup.sh` manages container creation, quadlet generation, and systemd setup
- **Follow Existing Patterns**: Copy structure from similar existing services
- **Port Allocation**: Use next available port in 8000-8888 range
- **Image Verification**: Check Docker Hub for correct image names, verify availability
- **Environment Variables**: Research and document all required env vars (PUID, PGID, TZ, etc.)
- **Volume Structure**: Plan required volumes including service-specific directories
- **Documentation**: Include comprehensive README.md with setup instructions
- **Testing**: Provide test procedures and health checks
- **Security Review**: Ensure no secrets are committed, use environment variables

## Service Structure Requirements
```
apps/new-service/
├── *.container              # AUTO-GENERATED: Podman quadlet (don't edit directly)
├── podman-setup.sh         # Service installation script (edit this)
├── environments/
│   ├── sample.env          # REQUIRED: Complete reference with ALL env vars documented
│   └── local.env           # Local overrides only (gitignored for sensitive services)
├── configs/                # Service-specific configurations
├── volumes/                # Persistent data (gitignored)
├── scripts/                # Helper scripts (init, rootless data, entrypoints)
└── README.md               # Setup and usage documentation
```

### Sample Environment File Convention
Every service MUST have a `sample.env` following the pattern in `apps/filebrowser/environments/sample.env`:
- Header with image name, docs URL, and copy instruction
- Variables grouped into `# ── Section ──` headers
- Every possible env var documented with default value and description
- Commented-out variables use defaults; uncommented are commonly overridden
- Never include real secrets — use placeholder values like `changeme`
- `local.env` contains ONLY values that differ from sample.env defaults

**Important**: Edit `podman-setup.sh` and run it to generate `.container` files automatically.

## Media Service Volume Convention
- Media services (Radarr, Sonarr, Lidarr, Readarr) mount `$HOME/media:/media` for hardlinking support
- Download clients mount `$HOME/media/Downloads:/downloads`
- Library paths and download paths must share the same parent mount for hardlinks to work
- See `docs/MEDIA_SERVER_GUIDE.md` for the complete directory structure

## Environment Setup Prerequisites
- **Podman**: Latest version with rootless container support
- **Systemd**: User services enabled for automatic management
- **Tailscale**: VPN client for secure remote access
- **SSL Certificates**: Self-signed certificates for local development
- **Environment Files**: Copy sample.env to local.env and configure

## Quality Standards
- **Error Handling**: Include proper error handling in scripts
- **Logging**: Use consistent logging format across all scripts
- **Resource Limits**: Set appropriate CPU/memory limits
- **Health Checks**: Include health checks where possible
- **Documentation**: Update all relevant documentation

## Common Pitfalls to Avoid
- **Don't assume LinuxServer images exist**: Always verify image availability on Docker Hub
- **Don't assume user handling method**: Check documentation for PUID/PGID vs `--user` flag requirements
- **Don't skip environment variables**: Research and include all mandatory env vars (TZ, PUID, PGID)
- **Don't ignore service-specific volumes**: Some services need special directory structures
- **Don't test endpoints without checking logs**: Always verify service is running via logs first

## HTTPS and SSL Considerations
- **Check Service Requirements**: Some services (like LinuxServer Calibre) require HTTPS by default
- **SSL Certificate Strategy**: Decide between reverse proxy with SSL certificates vs direct LAN access
- **Reverse Proxy Trade-offs**: Provides security layer but requires SSL certificates for HTTPS upstream
- **Direct LAN Access**: Simpler for local networks but exposes ports to entire LAN
- **Authentication**: Ensure services have built-in authentication when not behind reverse proxy

## Testing Requirements
- **Local Deployment**: Test container deployment locally first
- **Integration Testing**: Verify service integration with NGINX
- **Security Testing**: Ensure no security vulnerabilities
- **Performance Testing**: Validate resource usage under load

## Review Process
- **Code Review**: All changes require review before merging
- **Security Review**: Special attention to security implications
- **Documentation Review**: Ensure documentation is complete and accurate
- **Testing Verification**: Confirm all tests pass and functionality works